Summary: libaoahid is MIT. Its only dependency, libusb 1.0, is LGPL-2.1-or-later
and is always linked dynamically, so redistributing an unmodified release
archive satisfies both licenses. Details follow.

This distribution includes software developed by the libaoahid contributors
and is licensed under the MIT License. The complete license text is in LICENSE.

libaoahid uses libusb 1.0, which is a separate work distributed under the GNU
Lesser General Public License, version 2.1 or (at your option) any later version.
Windows packages include the dynamically linked libusb runtime and import
library, and macOS packages include `libusb-1.0.0.dylib`; the corresponding
libusb license and copyright file is included.
Static libaoahid archives do not incorporate libusb object code, but programs
linking those archives must provide a compatible libusb implementation.

The exact dependency version is recorded in each package's build metadata and
SPDX document. Every binary archive also carries the official libusb 1.0.30
source tarball and its verified SHA-256 under
share/doc/libaoahid/third-party/source/. Windows archives additionally carry
the exact pinned vcpkg libusb `vcpkg.json` and `portfile.cmake` files used by
the build. Those vcpkg-authored files are covered by Microsoft's MIT License;
the exact pinned `LICENSE.txt` is included as
share/doc/libaoahid/third-party/vcpkg-LICENSE.txt. The copied third-party
notices are authoritative for the packaged dependency and build-material
revisions.

Every asset that ships the libusb runtime carries this NOTICE, LICENSE,
THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md, libusb's LGPL-2.1 text, and libusb's corresponding
source archive in the same distribution unit. That includes the smaller
`*-runtime` bundles as well as the complete `*-shared` and `*-static`
archives. No bare shared object or DLL is published as a standalone release
asset, so no distributed unit containing the libusb runtime can become
separated from libusb's license and corresponding source.

Every first-party source file in this repository carries an
SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT tag and copyright line of its own, so a file
copied out of the repository remains identifiable without the root LICENSE.
The SPDX document in each release records the license of each packaged file
individually: MIT for first-party output, LGPL-2.1-or-later for every file
installed from libusb, and MIT for the pinned vcpkg material.

